---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import StepNav from "../components/StepNav.astro";
import PhotoCard from "../components/academy/PhotoCard.astro";

// Rule of Thirds — card shows the photo, modal shows the grid-overlay version
import ROT1 from "../assets/_DSC9072.jpg";
import ROT2 from "../assets/_DSC9072ROT.jpg";
// Centered Composition & Symmetry — card photo, modal grid-overlay version
import ROT3 from "../assets/_DSC6779.jpg";
import ROT4 from "../assets/_DSC6779ROT.jpg";
// Foreground Interest (lighthouse image removed; waterfall kept)
import Foreground2 from "../assets/20190504_10086DSC_5956.jpg";
// Frame within the Frame
import FrameinFrame from "../assets/20160417-_DSC4419.jpg";
// Leading Lines
import LeadingLines from "../assets/20190322_9005DSC_4880_1.jpg";
// Fill the Frame (statue image removed; owl kept)
import fillFrame2 from "../assets/DSC_8076.jpg";
// Isolate the Subject
import Isolate from "../assets/20170722-_DSC9588.jpg";
// Leave Negative Space
import Negativespace from "../assets/20210403_1213_DSC_9516.jpg";
// Rule of Space
import Ruleofspace from "../assets/_DSC2333.jpg";
// Change your Point of View
import Pointofview from "../assets/20190421_9784DSC_5659.jpg";
// Left to Right Rule
import Seanmotion from "../assets/seanmotion.jpg";
// Golden Ratio — card photo, modal Phi-grid overlay version
import Goldenratio1 from "../assets/goldenratio.jpg";
import Goldenratio2 from "../assets/goldenratiooverlay.jpg";
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Step 4: The Subject IS the Subject"
	description="Composition and subject — making the subject the subject of your photograph."
>
	<section class="wrapper">
		<h1 class="page-title">THE SUBJECT IS THE SUBJECT</h1>

		<p class="text">
			The subject of this subject is the subject. Images can depict anything and
			there is a way to compose them that will be pleasing for others too.
			Composition is the structure of an image. Even if the exposure is spot on,
			if the composition is off or unpleasing, your image will be weak. At it's
			most basic points, lines, and shapes are the elements of composition.
			Composition should be deliberate. Elements and lines should be intentionally
			placed in an image. I'm a big fan of the saying "Plan your work and work
			your plan". This means that you should know what you want to create an image
			of before you create the image. You will have an opportunity to think about
			how to compose your image and what you want the image to convey. Time of day
			and environmental setting are important factors to consider. The type of
			light, and equipment you will have available, and how long it will take you
			to set up or prepare to create the image. Deciding about all of the prep
			work will also help in deciding how and where to place items in your image to
			make a strong composition. Below is a list of things to consider about
			composing your image. They aren't hard and fast rules, they are guidelines
			and are meant to help make a strong composition.
		</p>

		<p class="deck-hint">Tap a card to see the technique and an example.</p>

		<div class="card-grid">
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="rule-of-thirds-dialog"
				title="Rule of Thirds"
				src={ROT1}
				alt="An oystercatcher wading bird standing on a sandy beach with the ocean and sky behind it"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="centered-composition-dialog"
				title="Centered Composition"
				src={ROT3}
				alt="Hopetoun House mansion in Queensferry, Scotland, mirrored in the still water in front of it"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="foreground-interest-dialog"
				title="Foreground Interest"
				src={Foreground2}
				alt="A waterfall with foreground rocks that add depth and lead toward the main subject"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="frame-within-frame-dialog"
				title="Frame Within the Frame"
				src={FrameinFrame}
				alt="Arches of a building forming a frame within the frame around the scene beyond"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="leading-lines-dialog"
				title="Leading Lines"
				src={LeadingLines}
				alt="A monument with the moon above it, its walls and lines leading attention toward the subject"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="fill-the-frame-dialog"
				title="Fill the Frame"
				src={fillFrame2}
				alt="An owl's face filling the frame, focusing attention on the subject with no distractions"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="isolate-subject-dialog"
				title="Isolate the Subject"
				src={Isolate}
				alt="A bird on a branch isolated against a blurred background using a shallow depth of field"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="negative-space-dialog"
				title="Leave Negative Space"
				src={Negativespace}
				alt="A single bird surrounded by empty sky, using negative space for a simple, minimal composition"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="point-of-view-dialog"
				title="Change Your Point of View"
				src={Pointofview}
				alt="Baby swans (cygnets) in the water photographed from a low point of view at their level"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="rule-of-space-dialog"
				title="Rule of Space"
				src={Ruleofspace}
				alt="A bird flying with more space left in front of it so it can move into the frame"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="left-to-right-dialog"
				title="Left to Right Rule"
				src={Seanmotion}
				alt="A child running from left to right while another child and a tree anchor the left side of the frame"
			/>
			<PhotoCard
				dialogId="golden-ratio-dialog"
				title="Golden Ratio"
				src={Goldenratio1}
				alt="A red fox sitting in the grass"
			/>
		</div>

		<StepNav prev={{ href: "/step3" }} />
	</section>

	<!-- ===== Lesson modals ===== -->
	<dialog id="rule-of-thirds-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Rule of Thirds</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={ROT2}
			alt="The beach photo with a rule-of-thirds grid overlaid, the bird placed on the right vertical line"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Divide the frame into 9 equal rectangles, 3 across and 3 down place the
			important element(s) of the image along one or more of the lines or where
			the lines intersect. This image actually uses the Rule of Thirds both
			horizontally with the sand, ocean and sky and vertically placing the bird
			(Oyster Catcher) on a vertical line on the right. It also uses the Rule of
			Space and breaks the Left to Right rule.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="centered-composition-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Centered Composition and Symmetry</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={ROT4}
			alt="The Hopetoun House photo with a rule-of-thirds grid overlaid, showing its symmetry"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Symmetrical scenes are perfect for a centered composition. Scenes containing
			reflections are also an opportunity to use symmetry in the composition. Try a
			mix of the rule of thirds and symmetry to compose the image. The image of the
			Hopetoun House in Queensferry Scotland uses the Rule of Thirds well and it is
			also perfectly symmetrical.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="foreground-interest-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Foreground Interest</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={Foreground2}
			alt="A waterfall with foreground rocks that add depth and lead toward the main subject"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Good for showing and creating space for the subject in your image to occupy.
			Including foreground interest is a way of adding depth to the scene. In this
			image the rocks in the foreground add depth and lead the viewer to the main
			subject.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="frame-within-frame-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Frame Within the Frame</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={FrameinFrame}
			alt="Arches of a building forming a frame within the frame around the scene beyond"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Including a frame within the frame is a way of portraying depth. Look for
			elements such as windows, arches or overhanging tree branches to frame the
			image with.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="leading-lines-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Leading Lines</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={LeadingLines}
			alt="A monument with the moon above it, its walls and lines leading attention toward the subject"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Leading lines help focus attention on important elements. Paths, walls or
			patterns can be leading lines and they do not have to be straight.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="fill-the-frame-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Fill the Frame</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={fillFrame2}
			alt="An owl's face filling the frame, focusing attention on the subject with no distractions"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Filling the frame with your subject, leaving little or no space around it can
			be effective. It helps focus the viewer completely on the main subject
			without any distractions.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="isolate-subject-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Isolate the Subject</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={Isolate}
			alt="A bird on a branch isolated against a blurred background using a shallow depth of field"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Using a shallow depth of field to isolate your subject will focus attention
			on the subject.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="negative-space-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Leave Negative Space</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={Negativespace}
			alt="A single bird surrounded by empty sky, using negative space for a simple, minimal composition"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Leaving a lot of empty or 'negative' space around your subject creates a
			sense of simplicity and minimalism.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="point-of-view-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Change Your Point of View</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={Pointofview}
			alt="Baby swans (cygnets) in the water photographed from a low point of view at their level"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Try getting high up or low down or shot from a different angle to compose
			your image. Study your subject to find what is pleasing and interesting.
			Sometimes getting on the same level is the best angle.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="rule-of-space-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Rule of Space</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={Ruleofspace}
			alt="A bird flying with more space left in front of it so it can move into the frame"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			The rule of space relates to the direction the subject(s) in your photo are
			facing. Leave more space in front of the subject so that it can "move" into
			it.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="left-to-right-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Left to Right Rule</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={Seanmotion}
			alt="A child running from left to right while another child and a tree anchor the left side of the frame"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			There is a theory that says we read an image from left to right in the same
			way we would read a book. Here my bionic son is running from left to right
			while my daughter and the tree anchor the left side of the image.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>

	<dialog id="golden-ratio-dialog" class="info-dialog">
		<h2>Golden Ratio</h2>
		<Image
			class="modal-img"
			src={Goldenratio2}
			alt="The red fox photo overlaid with a golden-ratio Phi grid and Fibonacci spiral, the fox's eye at the tightest point of the spiral"
			widths={[480, 960]}
			sizes="(max-width: 720px) 90vw, 680px"
		/>
		<p>
			Two quantities are in the golden ratio if their ratio is the same as the
			ratio of their sum to the larger of the two quantities. The frame is divided
			into a series of squares known as a "Phi Grid". Use the squares to draw a
			spiral called a "Fibonacci Spiral". It is believed that the golden spiral
			method of composition has been in existence for over 2,400 years having been
			devised in Ancient Greece. Notice how the eyes of the subject fall right in
			the smallest box and tightest area of the spiral.
		</p>
		<button type="button" class="dialog-close">Close</button>
	</dialog>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.wrapper {
		padding: 0.5em;
	}

	.text {
		font-size: 1.15em;
	}

	.deck-hint {
		text-align: center;
		color: #5b636b;
		font-size: 1em;
		margin-top: 1.5em;
	}

	/* Gallery of composition cards. */
	.card-grid {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(220px, 1fr));
		gap: 1rem;
		margin: 0.75rem 0 2rem;
	}

	/* Lesson dialogs. */
	.info-dialog {
		max-width: min(72ch, 92vw);
		max-height: 88vh;
		overflow: auto;
		border: 1px solid #ccc;
		border-radius: 6px;
		padding: 1.5rem;
		line-height: 1.6;
	}
	.info-dialog::backdrop {
		background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.5);
	}
	.info-dialog h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}
	.info-dialog p {
		font-size: 1.05em;
	}
	.modal-img {
		display: block;
		width: 100%;
		height: auto;
		margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
		border-radius: 4px;
	}
	.dialog-close {
		margin-top: 0.5rem;
		padding: 0.5rem 1.1rem;
		border: none;
		border-radius: 6px;
		background: var(--nav-bg);
		color: #fff;
		font-size: 1rem;
		cursor: pointer;
	}
</style>

<script>
	// Open the matching <dialog> from its card, and close via the button or a
	// click on the backdrop (native <dialog> handles Esc + focus trapping).
	document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>("[data-open-dialog]").forEach((btn) => {
		btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
			const id = btn.getAttribute("data-open-dialog");
			const dlg = id ? document.getElementById(id) : null;
			if (dlg instanceof HTMLDialogElement) dlg.showModal();
		});
	});
	document.querySelectorAll<HTMLDialogElement>("dialog.info-dialog").forEach((dlg) => {
		dlg.querySelector(".dialog-close")?.addEventListener("click", () => dlg.close());
		dlg.addEventListener("click", (e) => {
			if (e.target === dlg) dlg.close();
		});
	});
</script>
